      "math_academy_id": "q-160305",
      "sequence_position": 17,
      "problem": "What is the distance between the points $(1,-3)$  and $(5,-3)?$",
      "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224132/assets/b3a34b10cf329cea0e80959562484c07316a582d55395f8bf7aa951d2aca8567.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224132/assets/15afca52169a74eb433654bc992cc3d19e191a92704d7d845d0c49071800052e.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224132/assets/7298280773fb08b221742571e76ba5f422a77a2e70abd4b94085d602a196e8a7.png)\n\nThe point $(1,-3)$ is at a distance of $1$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(5,-3)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5-1=4.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
